d}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f88d75-56fc-5830-abd9-8918045eb899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323602} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec84d28-276b-55d4-b8cc-6076ca3122fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323603} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed552b7-4a5c-5857-8596-4be1bfd1eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323604} {:answer/id #uuid "de0660c8-d521-5633-b919-aebad0d3d770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323605} {:answer/id #uuid "9027aa4f-8d07-5ccd-89de-91f838767a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed552b7-4a5c-5857-8596-4be1bfd1eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323604} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaffc6b1-18c1-5c41-b5b7-ae198f92e213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de94dd8c-d1ac-5e3c-a7d7-a9749972614e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74151" :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k$, if the distance between the vectors is $\\text{d}(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the distance between $x$ and $y$ in terms of $k:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{y}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{y}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-k)^{2}+(-k-(-1))^{2}+(2k-0)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-6k+{k}^{2}+{k}^{2}-2k+1+4{k}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given equation  $\\text{d}(x,y)=2\\sqrt{3},$ we obtain an equation that we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14} & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6{k}^{2}-8k+14})}^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2} \\\\ 6{k}^{2}-8k+14 & =12 \\\\ 6{k}^{2}-8k+2 & =0 \\\\ 3{k}^{2}-4k+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quadratic formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(3)(1)}}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{4}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $k=1$ and $k=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}]}
